    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_edges_by_id - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_edges_by_id.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_edges_by_id()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add multiple edges using integer node IDs.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_edges_by_id()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add multiple edges using integer node IDs.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_edges_by_id()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Add multiple edges using integer node IDs.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd multiple edges using integer node IDs.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L2332-L2502){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_edges_by_id(\n    edges,\n    rel=None,\n    weight=None,\n    **shared_attrs,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Add several edges by ID](../assets/graphs/add-edges-by-id.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Before: four nodes tagged with their integer ids, A 1, B 2, C 3, D 4, with two edges, B to A to C; the ids 2, 3, and 4 are highlighted. After: the id pairs (2, 4) and (3, 4) become two new accent edges, B to D and C to D, closing the cycle.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA strict bulk version of `add_edge_by_id()` that only accepts integer node IDs — no\nlabel resolution. All referenced nodes must already exist in the graph.\n\nThis is the ID-based counterpart of `add_edges()`. It is useful when edges are generated\nprogrammatically from node IDs (for example from `get_node_ids()` or the `id` column of a\nnode DataFrame) and you want to be sure no value is accidentally interpreted as a label.\nEdges are added in list order, each with its own edge ID, and the call is recorded as a\nsingle step in the step log (see `steps`).\n\nEdges are added one at a time, so if an invalid ID is encountered part-way through the\nlist, the edges before it have already been added when the error is raised.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[edges]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Edge definitions to add. Each element can be:\n\n    - A tuple of `(source_id, target_id)` with shared attributes applied.\n    - A tuple of `(source_id, target_id, attrs_dict)` to override shared attributes. The\n      dict is merged on top of `**shared_attrs=` and may also contain `\"rel\"` and\n      `\"weight\"` keys to override `rel=` and `weight=` for that edge.\n\n    Both IDs must be Python `int` values referring to existing nodes.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional relationship type applied to every edge (unless overridden per-edge). Stored\n    in the first-class `rel` column of the edge DataFrame. Default is `None`.\n\n<code>[weight]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional numeric weight applied to every edge (unless overridden per-edge). Stored\n    whether or not the graph was created with `weighted=True`. Default is `None`.\n\n<code>[**shared_attrs]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>\n\n:   Attributes applied to every edge (unless overridden per-edge). Each key becomes a\n    column in the edge DataFrame.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Returns self to enable method chaining.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [TypeError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If any source or target is not an integer.\n\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If any source or target node ID does not exist in the graph.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_edges`) \n\n:   Add edges by label or ID.\n\n[](`~add_edge_by_id`) \n\n:   Add one edge between node IDs.\n\n[](`~select_last_edges_created`) \n\n:   Select the edges just added.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nLook up the IDs, then add a chain of edges by ID with a shared `rel`:\n\n::: {#d2ba9152 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_nodes([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"])\na, b, c, d = (graph.get_id_from_label(x) for x in [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"])\n\ngraph.add_edges_by_id([(a, b), (b, c), (c, d)], rel=\"link\")\n\ngraph.get_edge_df()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (3, 4)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;link&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>&quot;link&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>&quot;link&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA dict as the third tuple element overrides the shared values for that edge only, so the\nY-Z edge below is `\"strong\"` while X-Y keeps the shared `\"weak\"`:\n\n::: {#f4e73c96 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_nodes([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nx, y, z = (graph.get_id_from_label(label) for label in [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\n\ngraph.add_edges_by_id([(x, y), (y, z, {\"rel\": \"strong\"})], rel=\"weak\")\n\ngraph.get_edge_df()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (2, 4)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;weak&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>&quot;strong&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nBecause the input is plain integer pairs, edge lists can be generated directly from node\nIDs. Here the five IDs returned by `get_node_ids()` are joined into a ring, giving one edge\nper node:\n\n::: {#1bbd6529 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_nodes(range(1, 6))\nnode_ids = graph.get_node_ids()\n\ngraph.add_edges_by_id(list(zip(node_ids, node_ids[1:] + node_ids[:1])))\n\ngraph.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n5\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Next month's wages\n\nIn `payments()`, Acme pays wages to four people. `get_successors()` gives their IDs, so\nnext month's payroll is one call, with the date shared by every payment:\n\n::: {#cb8a8800 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments = ec.payments()\nacme = payments.get_id_from_label(\"Acme\")\n\nemployees = payments.get_successors(acme)\npayroll = [(acme, employee, 2500) for employee in employees]\npayments.add_edges_by_id(payroll, date=\"2026-04-01\")\n\npayments.get_out_degree(\"Acme\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n8\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n`select_last_edges_created()` selects the four new payments, and\n`highlight_edges=\"selection\"` draws them:\n\n::: {#bac76100 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments.select_last_edges_created(len(payroll))\n\npayments.plot(highlight_edges=\"selection\", label_by=\"label\", seed=6)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ec